From faccdc7518a86dd87127381ed0a45a01a225e928 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 20:33:56 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 072/111] particle: sprite orientation (screen / upright /
 fixed, axis, flags)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WE's sprite renderer reads `orientation` (screen 0, upright 1, fixed 2), `axis` (default
"0 0 0" = y, normalised) and `flags` (GH 0x1401efc50), and every frame turns them into
g_OrientationForward/Right/Up (GH 0x14024fd30): forward and up are chosen in the world —
screen: −view forward and the view's up (flags bit 0) or the system's y; upright: world y
(bit 0) or the system's axis, right = view forward × up, forward = right × up; fixed: the
axis and its in-plane up, as given (bit 0) or the system's — then taken into the system's
space by the transpose of its model matrix and normalised, right = up × forward.

LWE always drew the system's own axes: a system tilted about x foreshortened its sprites, a
fixed sprite faced the camera, and a child system copied its parent's orientation instead of
using its own renderer. 3174556087's rain splashes (upright and fixed, flags 1, in squashed,
turned systems) now follow WE's axes. Ropes and trails keep LWE's identity (their renderers'
parsers are not read). With no rotation every mode is the identity LWE drew before.

mapsequencearoundcontrolpoint: WE's defaults are bounds "0 1", limitbehavior "repeat", axis
"0 0 1" (GH 0x1401e8c90), which is what LWE does; the library uses only those. Other values
are reported to the health report (their runtime is in the operator interpreter, not read).

Origin: ours
Why: docs/player/spikes/we-coverage.md F9 (P29, P27)
Status: candidate-for-upstream
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
